FIG. 9. The mod N addition operator ADDN (a, N ) computes a + b (mod N ), where a is a
K-bit c-number and b is a K-bit q-number. When ADDN is enabled, the comparison operator
XLT (N − a) flips the value of the select bit to ℓ = 1 if a + b < N ; then the multiplexed adder
M ADD(2K + a − N, a) chooses the c-number addend to be a for ℓ = 1 and 2K + a − N for ℓ = 0.
XLT uses and then clears K-bits of scratch space before M ADD writes the mod N sum there.

